`FixedGrid::advance` adds elapsed milliseconds to the unconsumed remainder and performs one simulation step per complete `step_ms` interval. The quotient and remainder must reflect the mathematical sum without saturation loss, including near `u64::MAX`. It returns the exact step count as `usize`; under the stated 64-bit target and maintained remainder invariant, that count is representable.

Every step applies the same borrowed held input. Opposing directions cancel independently on each axis. Otherwise coordinates move by one per step and remain clamped to the exclusive ranges `0..width` and `0..height`. Bulk movement is permitted if equivalent to repeated steps and does not require a huge loop or grid allocation. Zero elapsed time performs no new steps, an exact interval performs one step, and partial time carries into later calls. The input must not be mutated.

`step_ms`, `width`, and `height` must be positive. `new` returns `ZeroStep`, then `ZeroWidth`, then `ZeroHeight` in that validation order.

Known-bad source:
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]
pub struct HeldInput {
    pub left: bool,
    pub right: bool,
    pub up: bool,
    pub down: bool,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum GridError {
    ZeroStep,
    ZeroWidth,
    ZeroHeight,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct FixedGrid {
    pub x: usize,
    pub y: usize,
    width: usize,
    height: usize,
    step_ms: u64,
    accumulated_ms: u64,
}

impl FixedGrid {
    pub fn new(width: usize, height: usize, step_ms: u64) -> Result<Self, GridError> {
        if step_ms == 0 {
            return Err(GridError::ZeroStep);
        }
        if width == 0 {
            return Err(GridError::ZeroWidth);
        }
        if height == 0 {
            return Err(GridError::ZeroHeight);
        }
        Ok(Self { x: 0, y: 0, width, height, step_ms, accumulated_ms: 0 })
    }

    pub fn advance(&mut self, elapsed_ms: u64, input: &HeldInput) -> usize {
        self.accumulated_ms = self.accumulated_ms.saturating_add(elapsed_ms);
        let mut steps = 0;
        while self.accumulated_ms >= self.step_ms {
            self.accumulated_ms -= self.step_ms;
            if input.left != input.right {
                if input.left {
                    self.x = self.x.saturating_sub(1);
                } else {
                    self.x = (self.x + 1).min(self.width - 1);
                }
            }
            if input.up != input.down {
                if input.up {
                    self.y = self.y.saturating_sub(1);
                } else {
                    self.y = (self.y + 1).min(self.height - 1);
                }
            }
            steps += 1;
        }
        steps
    }
}
```

Exact-interval handling is already correct. The remaining defect is the saturating elapsed-time addition, which can discard carried time.

